export const FRAUD_LAW: Readonly<Record<string, FraudLaw>> = JSON.parse("{\"US\":{\"source\":\"https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1005/6/; https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1005/11/; https://www.consumerfinance.gov/rules-policy/regulations/1026/12/; https://bjs.ojp.gov/document/vit21.pdf, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"withinDays\":2,\"max\":5000},{\"withinDays\":60,\"max\":50000},{}],\"debitWords\":\"Under the Electronic Fund Transfer Act's Regulation E, 12 CFR 1005.6(b), a consumer who reports a lost or stolen debit card within two business days of learning of it owes at most the lesser of $50 or the transfers made before notice; after two business days the cap rises to $500. Transfers shown on a periodic statement that aren't reported within 60 days of the statement being sent carry unlimited liability for transfers after that 60-day window. The 2- and 60-day clocks run from different starts: learning of the loss, and the statement's transmittal.\",\"creditMax\":5000,\"creditWords\":\"Truth in Lending Act, Regulation Z, 12 CFR 1026.12(b)(1)(ii): a cardholder's liability for unauthorized credit card use is capped at the lesser of $50 or the amount obtained before the card issuer is notified.\",\"refundDays\":10,\"refundWords\":\"Regulation E, 12 CFR 1005.11: the bank must decide within 10 business days (20 for a new account's first 30 days); if it takes longer it must provisionally credit the account within 10 business days and may take up to 45 days, or 90 days for point-of-sale debit card, out-of-state or new-account transfers. Results go to the consumer within 3 business days of finishing.\",\"claimDays\":60,\"fraudRate\":0.04,\"fraudBasis\":\"BJS, Victims of Identity Theft, 2021 (NCVS Identity Theft Supplement): 'almost 4%' of U.S. residents age 16 or older had at least one incident of misuse of an existing credit card in the past 12 months, and 3% had misuse of an existing bank account (which includes debit cards). 7,289,970 people (2.2%) had only credit card misuse as their most recent incident, out of 263 million persons 16+. Credit card figure used; bank-account misuse is on top of it and overlaps.\",\"avgLoss\":62000,\"avgLossBasis\":\"BJS Victims of Identity Theft, 2021, Table 6: mean direct loss of $620 among victims whose most recent incident was misuse of an existing credit card only and who lost $1 or more (the median was $200, and 78% of those victims lost money). This is the loss before any reimbursement by the card issuer.\"},\"CA\":{\"source\":\"https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/industry/laws-regulations/debit-card-code-conduct.html; https://laws-lois.justice.gc.ca/eng/acts/B-1.01/section-627.33.html; https://www.canada.ca/en/financial-consumer-agency/services/rights-responsibilities/protection-unauthorized-transactions.html; https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/pub/89-652-x/89-652-x2023001-eng.htm, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":0}],\"debitWords\":\"Debit cards fall under the voluntary Canadian Code of Practice for Consumer Debit Card Services (section 5): the cardholder isn't liable for losses from circumstances beyond their control, including being the victim of fraud, theft or coercion, technical failures, and use after the card is reported lost or stolen. A cardholder who contributed (for example by voluntarily sharing a PIN or not reporting in reasonable time) can be liable, but no more than the card's withdrawal limits, and more only where overdraft or a linked credit line is attached. There’s no statutory dollar cap for debit.\",\"creditMax\":5000,\"creditWords\":\"Bank Act, S.C. 1991, c. 46, s. 627.33: a borrower's maximum liability for unauthorized use of a credit card issued in Canada is $50 unless they showed gross negligence (gross fault in Quebec), and nothing after they report the card lost, stolen or at risk. Use of the PIN alone doesn't prove gross negligence. For non-bank federally regulated issuers, FCAC says the cap is the lesser of $50 or the agreement's amount.\",\"refundDays\":10,\"refundWords\":\"Debit Card Code of Practice: the issuer must respond to a report of an unauthorized transaction as soon as possible and in no more than 10 business days, a limit it can pause while waiting for a signed statement or affidavit. No statutory refund deadline is set for credit cards.\",\"fraudRate\":0.024,\"fraudBasis\":\"Statistics Canada, General Social Survey on Canadians' Safety 2019 (Self-reported fraud in Canada, 2019): 12% of the population 15+ had someone use their personal or banking information to obtain money or buy goods in the five years before the survey. 12% ÷ 5 years = about 2.4% a year. (The same survey found 7.8% were victims of any fraud in the previous 12 months, which also counts scams.)\"},\"MX\":{\"source\":\"https://leyes-mx.com/ley_para_la_transparencia_y_ordenamiento_de_los_servicios_financieros/23.htm; https://www.gob.mx/condusef/prensa/cargos-no-reconocidos-en-tarjeta-de-debito-se-restituiran-en-dos-dias-habiles-bancarios?idiom=es; https://www.gob.mx/condusef/articulos/cargos-no-reconocidos?idiom=es; https://www.cnbv.gob.mx/Inclusi%C3%B3n/Anexos%20Inclusin%20Financiera/Reporte_ENIF2024.pdf, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"withinDays\":2,\"max\":0},{}],\"debitWords\":\"Banco de México's card rules, in force since 2019 as CONDUSEF describes them, require a bank told of unrecognized charges to credit the account by the second banking business day for transactions made in the 48 hours before the report. Older charges go through the general dispute (aclaración) process of Article 23 of the Ley para la Transparencia y Ordenamiento de los Servicios Financieros, which sets no peso cap on the customer's loss.\",\"creditWords\":\"No statutory peso cap found. The same Banco de México rules (48-hour window, credit by the second business day) apply to credit cards, and under Article 23 of the Ley para la Transparencia y Ordenamiento de los Servicios Financieros a cardholder may withhold payment of a disputed charge, without interest or late fees and without being reported to a credit bureau, until the claim is resolved.\",\"refundDays\":2,\"refundWords\":\"Charges from the 48 hours before the report are credited by the second banking business day (Banco de México rules, per CONDUSEF). Under Article 23 LTOSF the institution must answer a dispute within 45 days, or 180 days for foreign transactions; CONDUSEF says an unanswered claim is treated as resolved in the customer's favor.\",\"claimDays\":90,\"fraudRate\":0.02,\"fraudBasis\":\"INEGI/CNBV, Encuesta Nacional de Inclusión Financiera 2024: 6% of adults (18–70) who have or had a financial product reported card cloning (clonación de tarjeta de débito o crédito) in the last three years. 6% ÷ 3 years = 2% a year.\"},\"GB\":{\"source\":\"https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2017/752/regulation/74; https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2017/752/regulation/76; https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2017/752/regulation/77; https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1974/39/section/84; UK Finance Annual Fraud Report 2026, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":3500}],\"debitWords\":\"Payment Services Regulations 2017, reg. 77: the payer can be made to bear up to £35 of losses from a lost, stolen or misappropriated payment instrument, but nothing if the loss wasn't detectable before the payment, was caused by the provider's staff or agents, happened after notification, or strong customer authentication was required and not applied. A payer who acted fraudulently or failed their obligations with intent or gross negligence bears all the losses.\",\"creditMax\":3500,\"creditWords\":\"Consumer Credit Act 1974, s. 84(1): a debtor under a credit-token (credit card) agreement can be made liable for misuse only up to £35, or the credit limit if lower, and s. 84(3) ends even that once the issuer has been told the card is lost or stolen. The PSRs 2017 reg. 77 cap of £35 also applies to cards as payment instruments.\",\"refundDays\":1,\"refundWords\":\"PSRs 2017, reg. 76: the provider must refund as soon as practicable and no later than the end of the business day after it becomes aware of the unauthorized transaction, unless it has reasonable grounds to suspect the customer of fraud and notifies the authorities under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002.\",\"claimDays\":396,\"fraudRate\":0.022,\"fraudBasis\":\"UK Finance Annual Fraud Report 2026: 3,775,687 confirmed unauthorized card fraud cases in 2025 (cases are cards defrauded, not people) ÷ 169.2m UK debit and credit cards in issue at December 2025 (108,804k debit + 60,355k credit, UK Finance Card Spending Update) = 2.2% of cards a year. A person with several cards faces a higher chance.\",\"avgLoss\":15756,\"avgLossBasis\":\"UK Finance Annual Fraud Report 2026: 2025 unauthorized card fraud losses of £594.9m ÷ 3,775,687 cases (cards defrauded) = £157.56 per case.\",\"preventedShare\":0.6915,\"preventedBasis\":\"UK Finance Annual Fraud Report 2026, card fraud 2025: £1,333.3m prevented ÷ (£1,333.3m prevented + £594.9m losses) = 69.1% of attempted card fraud by value.\"},\"IE\":{\"source\":\"https://www.irishstatutebook.ie/eli/2018/si/6/made/en/print; https://www.cso.ie/en/releasesandpublications/ep/p-cv/crimeandvictimisation2025/personalcrime/, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":5000}],\"debitWords\":\"European Union (Payment Services) Regulations 2018 (S.I. No. 6 of 2018), reg. 98, transposing PSD2 Art. 74: the payer bears up to €50 of losses from a lost, stolen or misappropriated payment instrument, but nothing if the loss wasn't detectable before the payment, was caused by the provider's staff or agents, or strong customer authentication wasn't required. Fraud, or intentional or grossly negligent breach of the payer's obligations under reg. 93, removes the cap.\",\"creditMax\":5000,\"creditWords\":\"The same S.I. 6/2018 reg. 98 €50 cap covers credit cards, as they are payment instruments under PSD2.\",\"refundDays\":1,\"refundWords\":\"S.I. 6/2018, reg. 97(1): refund immediately and no later than the end of the business day after the provider notes or is notified of the transaction, unless it has reasonable grounds to suspect fraud and communicates them to the Central Bank.\",\"claimDays\":396,\"fraudRate\":0.05,\"fraudBasis\":\"CSO Crime and Victimisation 2025: 11% of persons aged 18+ were victims of fraud (including attempted) in the last 12 months, and card payment fraud affected 45% of fraud victims. 11% × 45% = 4.95% of adults a year.\",\"avgLoss\":10584,\"avgLossBasis\":\"BPFI FraudSMART Payment Fraud Report 2023 (the latest year with a card breakdown found): card fraud gross losses of €35.2m ÷ 332,576 fraudulent card transactions = €105.84; the report itself gives an average of €106. This is per fraudulent transaction, not per defrauded card, so it understates loss per case.\"},\"IS\":{\"source\":\"https://www.althingi.is/lagas/nuna/2021114.html; Eurostat isoc_cisci_pb, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":5000}],\"debitWords\":\"Lög um greiðsluþjónustu nr. 114/2021 (Payment Services Act), art. 80, Iceland's PSD2 transposition: the payer bears losses up to the equivalent of €50 in krónur, at the official mid-rate, from a lost, stolen or misused payment instrument, but nothing if they couldn't have noticed the loss, the provider's staff caused it, it happened after notice, or strong customer authentication wasn't required. Fraud, or intentional or grossly negligent breach of the art. 75 duties, removes the cap.\",\"creditMax\":5000,\"creditWords\":\"Art. 80 of Act 114/2021 applies to any payment instrument, credit cards included: up to the ISK equivalent of €50.\",\"refundDays\":1,\"refundWords\":\"Act 114/2021, art. 79: refund at once and no later than the end of the next business day after discovery or notice; a provider suspecting fraud may refuse and must report it to the Financial Supervisory Authority.\",\"claimDays\":396,\"fraudRate\":0.054,\"fraudBasis\":\"Eurostat isoc_cisci_pb, 2019 (latest year of this indicator): 5.4% of individuals aged 16–74 in Iceland experienced fraudulent credit or debit card use in the last 12 months (indicator I_SECFRD2, asked as a security problem met using the internet).\",\"avgLoss\":7600,\"avgLossCurrency\":\"EUR\",\"avgLossBasis\":\"The EEA-wide average, €76 a fraudulent card payment (EBA-ECB 2025 report on payment fraud); no figure for the country itself was found.\",\"inEuros\":true},\"FR\":{\"source\":\"https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/codes/article_lc/LEGIARTI000035430527; https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/codes/section_lc/LEGITEXT000006072026/LEGISCTA000020860778/; https://www.legifrance.gouv.fr/codes/article_lc/LEGIARTI000035430562; SSMSI VRS 2023, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":5000}],\"debitWords\":\"Code monétaire et financier, art. L133-19: before notice, the payer bears losses from a lost or stolen instrument up to €50, but nothing if the transaction was made without the personalized security data, the loss wasn't detectable, the provider's staff caused it, the card was used remotely without the payer's knowledge, it was counterfeited while in their possession, or strong authentication wasn't required. Fraud, or intentional or grossly negligent breach of arts. L133-16 and L133-17, makes the payer bear all losses.\",\"creditMax\":5000,\"creditWords\":\"Art. L133-19 CMF covers every payment instrument, credit cards included: €50 cap on the same terms.\",\"refundDays\":1,\"refundWords\":\"Code monétaire et financier, art. L133-18: refund at the latest by the end of the first business day after the provider learns of the unauthorized transaction.\",\"claimDays\":396,\"fraudRate\":0.047,\"fraudBasis\":\"SSMSI (Interior Ministry statistical service), survey Vécu et ressenti en matière de sécurité 2023: in 2022, 4.7% of persons 18+ said they were victims of fraudulent debits on a bank account (2,445,000 victims). Counts all fraudulent debits, not only card ones.\",\"avgLoss\":7099,\"avgLossBasis\":\"Banque de France OSMP annual report 2025, table T6 (cards issued in France), 2025: €474,541,183 of fraud ÷ 6,684,919 fraudulent transactions = €70.99 per fraudulent transaction. This is per transaction, not per defrauded card, so it understates loss per case.\"},\"DE\":{\"source\":\"https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/bgb/__675v.html; https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/bgb/__675u.html; https://www.gesetze-im-internet.de/bgb/__676b.html; Eurostat isoc_cisci_pb, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":5000}],\"debitWords\":\"Bürgerliches Gesetzbuch (BGB) §675v: the payment provider can claim up to €50 for losses from a lost, stolen or misused payment instrument, but nothing if the payer couldn't have noticed the loss, the provider's staff caused it, or the provider didn't require strong customer authentication. Fraudulent intent, or intentional or grossly negligent breach of §675l duties, makes the payer liable for the whole loss.\",\"creditMax\":5000,\"creditWords\":\"BGB §675v applies to every payment instrument (Zahlungsinstrument), credit cards included: €50 on the same terms.\",\"refundDays\":1,\"refundWords\":\"BGB §675u: refund without delay, at the latest by the end of the business day after the day the provider was told of or learned about the unauthorized transaction.\",\"claimDays\":396,\"fraudRate\":0.0098,\"fraudBasis\":\"Eurostat isoc_cisci_pb, 2019 (latest year of this indicator): 0.98% of individuals aged 16–74 in Germany experienced fraudulent credit or debit card use in the last 12 months (I_SECFRD2).\",\"avgLoss\":7600,\"avgLossCurrency\":\"EUR\",\"avgLossBasis\":\"The EEA-wide average, €76 a fraudulent card payment (EBA-ECB 2025 report on payment fraud); no figure for the country itself was found.\"},\"ES\":{\"source\":\"https://www.boe.es/buscar/act.php?id=BOE-A-2018-16036; Eurostat isoc_cisci_pb, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":5000}],\"debitWords\":\"Real Decreto-ley 19/2018, art. 46: the payer can be made to bear up to €50 of losses from a lost, stolen or misappropriated instrument, but nothing if the loss wasn't detectable or the provider's staff caused it, and nothing for card-not-present use of only the data printed on the card, for use after notice, or where strong customer authentication wasn't required. Fraud, or intentional or grossly negligent breach of art. 41 duties, removes the cap.\",\"creditMax\":5000,\"creditWords\":\"RDL 19/2018 art. 46 covers every payment instrument, credit cards included: €50 on the same terms.\",\"refundDays\":1,\"refundWords\":\"RDL 19/2018, art. 45: refund immediately and at the latest by the end of the business day after the transaction is noticed or notified, unless the provider has reasonable grounds to suspect fraud and tells the Bank of Spain in writing.\",\"claimDays\":396,\"fraudRate\":0.0312,\"fraudBasis\":\"Eurostat isoc_cisci_pb, 2019 (latest year of this indicator): 3.12% of individuals aged 16–74 in Spain experienced fraudulent credit or debit card use in the last 12 months (I_SECFRD2).\",\"avgLoss\":7600,\"avgLossCurrency\":\"EUR\",\"avgLossBasis\":\"The EEA-wide average, €76 a fraudulent card payment (EBA-ECB 2025 report on payment fraud); no figure for the country itself was found.\"},\"JP\":{\"source\":\"https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/api/1/lawdata/417AC1000000094 (Act No. 94 of 2005, arts. 3–5 and the two-year rule), 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"withinDays\":30,\"max\":0},{}],\"debitWords\":\"No law caps debit card purchase losses. The Depositor Protection Act (Act No. 94 of 2005) covers only ATM withdrawals with forged or stolen cash cards: forged-card withdrawals are void unless the depositor acted intentionally or with gross negligence (art. 4); for a stolen card the bank must repay withdrawals made from 30 days before the depositor's prompt notice (art. 5), in full, or three quarters if the bank proves ordinary negligence, and nothing for gross negligence or theft by a family member. Earlier withdrawals aren’t covered.\",\"creditWords\":\"No statutory cap on credit card liability was found for Japan; protection for unauthorized credit card use is set by each issuer's card member terms, not by law.\",\"refundWords\":\"The Depositor Protection Act sets no refund deadline.\",\"claimDays\":730},\"KR\":{\"source\":\"https://www.law.go.kr (여신전문금융업법 제16조; 시행령 제6조의9; 전자금융거래법 제9조, 제10조), 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{}],\"debitWords\":\"Electronic Financial Transactions Act art. 9: the bank must pay losses from forged or altered access media, errors in processing, and access media obtained by hacking into networks, unless the user acted intentionally or with gross negligence under a prior written agreement; art. 10 covers loss and theft of access media. The Specialized Credit Financial Business Act art. 16(1) makes the issuer liable for debit card use from the moment it’s told the card is lost or stolen. No statutory cap on a debit cardholder's loss before notice was found.\",\"creditMax\":0,\"creditWords\":\"Specialized Credit Financial Business Act art. 16(2), with Enforcement Decree art. 6-9(1): the card company is liable for credit card use in the 60 days before it's told of loss or theft, and for everything after. Under art. 16(3) a written contract can shift part or all of that to a cardholder at fault. Under art. 16(5) the issuer bears forged cards, data stolen by hacking and cards issued in someone else's name, and under art. 16(10) it can't collect a disputed amount until it has investigated.\",\"refundWords\":\"No statutory refund deadline was found; art. 16(10) bars collecting a disputed credit card amount until the investigation ends.\",\"claimDays\":60},\"AU\":{\"source\":\"https://download.asic.gov.au/media/lloeicwb/epayments-code-published-02-june-2022.pdf; https://www.abs.gov.au/statistics/people/crime-and-justice/personal-fraud/latest-release, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":15000}],\"debitWords\":\"ASIC's ePayments Code (June 2022), which covers ATM, EFTPOS, credit card, online and mobile payments: the holder isn't liable where it's clear they didn't contribute to the loss (cl. 10.3), or for fraud or negligence by bank staff, merchants or networks (cl. 10.1). Where a passcode was needed and fault can't be shown, liability is the least of $150, the balance available, or the loss before the report (cl. 11.7). If the bank proves the user contributed through fraud, a passcode breach or unreasonable delay, the holder bears actual losses before the report, within daily limits and the balance (cl. 11.2, 11.5).\",\"creditMax\":15000,\"creditWords\":\"The ePayments Code covers credit card transactions, so the same rules apply: $0 if clearly not at fault, and a $150 limit where fault can't be shown (cl. 11.7). Clause 11.10 also requires the issuer to try card-scheme chargeback rights.\",\"refundDays\":21,\"refundWords\":\"ePayments Code cl. 18: within 21 days of a report the bank must finish investigating or explain in writing that it needs more time, and it must finish within 45 days unless circumstances are exceptional. The code sets no separate refund deadline.\",\"claimDays\":2190,\"fraudRate\":0.1,\"fraudBasis\":\"ABS, Personal Fraud, 2024–25 financial year: an estimated 10% of persons aged 15 and over (2.3 million) experienced card fraud in the 12 months (9.9% in 2023–24). This is card fraud alone: ABS counts scams separately (2.7%, 596,600 persons).\",\"avgLoss\":25000,\"avgLossBasis\":\"ABS Personal Fraud 2024–25: the median amount taken in the most recent card fraud incident was $250 (a median, not a mean). Card fraud took $2.2 billion before reimbursement and $350 million after it; 75% of victims were reimbursed, 72% of them in full.\"},\"NZ\":{\"source\":\"https://nzba.org.nz/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/Code-of-Banking-Practice-A4-print.pdf; https://www.justice.govt.nz/justice-sector-policy/research-data/nzcvs/nzcvs-cycle-8-resources-and-results/, 2026-09-26\",\"debitTiers\":[{\"max\":0}],\"debitWords\":\"New Zealand Banking Association Code of Banking Practice (current edition, with scam commitments from 30 November 2025): the bank compensates losses from an unauthorized payment, meaning someone used your card or electronic banking without authority, if you weren't dishonest or negligent, took reasonable steps to protect your banking, and co-operate quickly. It's an industry code; no statute sets a dollar cap.\",\"creditMax\":0,\"creditWords\":\"The Code of Banking Practice's unauthorized-payment promise covers cards generally, credit cards included: full compensation if you weren't dishonest or negligent. No statutory cap applies.\",\"refundDays\":30,\"refundWords\":\"Code of Banking Practice: once the bank has the information it needs, it pays any compensation within 30 business days.\",\"fraudRate\":0.089,\"fraudBasis\":\"Ministry of Justice, NZ Crime and Victims Survey Cycle 8 (2025), prevalence table: 8.9% of adults (388,000) were victims of 'fraud and deception' in the 12 months. No card-only figure is published in the survey tables or reports read. This category also includes scams, so it overstates card-only fraud.\"}}");
